---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';

// Written against what the code actually does. If you change data handling —
// new cookies, new third-party requests, new stored fields — update this page
// in the same commit.
const UPDATED = '2026-08-28';
---

<PageLayout
	title="Privacy Policy"
	description="How UptimeCove handles data: no analytics, no tracking cookies, and a self-hosted architecture where your monitoring data never leaves your own Cloudflare account."
	heading="Privacy Policy"
	tagline="Short version: this site runs no analytics and sets no tracking cookies, and a self-hosted UptimeCove sends your monitoring data nowhere but your own Cloudflare account."
	updated={UPDATED}
>
	<section class="card p-6">
		<h2>1. Two different things called "UptimeCove"</h2>
		<p>This distinction decides who is responsible for your data, so it comes first:</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>The software.</strong> UptimeCove is an open-source project you download and deploy to
				your own Cloudflare account. It has no phone-home, no licence check and no telemetry. When you
				run it, <strong>you</strong> are the data controller for everything inside your instance — this
				policy cannot and does not speak for your deployment.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>This website</strong> (the marketing pages, the docs and the public demo). That's the
				only thing the sections below describe.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			If you reached a status page hosted by some other company or person, they run that instance. Ask
			them for their privacy policy — not us.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="card p-6">
		<h2>2. What this website collects</h2>

		<h3>Analytics: none</h3>
		<p>
			There is no Google Analytics, no Plausible, no PostHog, no Segment, no session recording and no
			advertising pixel anywhere on this site. Web fonts are self-hosted, so loading a page does not
			contact a font CDN either.
		</p>

		<h3>Cookies: one, and only if you log in</h3>
		<p>
			A single cookie named <code>uc_session</code> is set when an administrator signs in to a
			dashboard. It holds an opaque session identifier — no personal data is encoded in it. It is
			<code>HttpOnly</code>, <code>SameSite=Lax</code>, <code>Secure</code> in production, and expires
			after 7 days or when the session is revoked.
		</p>
		<p>
			It is strictly necessary for authentication, is never used for tracking or profiling, and is
			never shared. Browsing the public pages sets no cookies at all, which is why you don't see a
			consent banner.
		</p>

		<h3>Server logs</h3>
		<p>
			This site is hosted on Cloudflare Workers. Like any web host, Cloudflare processes request
			metadata — IP address, user agent, timestamp, requested URL — to route traffic and block abuse.
			That processing is governed by
			<a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cloudflare's privacy policy</a>.
			We do not build profiles from it or retain it in our own database.
		</p>

		<h3>Third-party requests</h3>
		<p>
			The landing page embeds a GitHub star-count badge from <code>img.shields.io</code>. Loading that
			image discloses your IP address and user agent to shields.io, the same as any other remote image
			would. It sets no cookies. No other third-party resource is requested by this site.
		</p>

		<h3>The demo dashboard</h3>
		<p>
			<a href="/demo">/demo</a> is populated with sample data and is read-only. Nothing you look at
			there is submitted, stored or associated with you.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="card p-6">
		<h2>3. What a self-hosted instance stores</h2>
		<p>
			Listed so you know exactly what lands in your own Cloudflare D1 database — and so self-hosters
			can write an accurate policy for their own users. All of it stays in your account.
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>Monitors and check results</strong> — URLs, HTTP status codes, response times, failure reasons.</li>
			<li><strong>Incidents and updates</strong> — open/close timestamps, causes, any notes an operator writes.</li>
			<li><strong>Status page subscribers</strong> — the email address someone submits to receive updates, the page they subscribed to, a verification flag and the signup timestamp. This is the only genuinely personal data the software collects by design.</li>
			<li><strong>Notification channel config</strong> — recipient addresses and webhook URLs for email, webhook, Slack and Discord alerts.</li>
			<li><strong>Sessions and API keys</strong> — hashed credentials and expiry timestamps. Raw tokens are never stored; keys are kept as SHA-256 hashes.</li>
			<li><strong>Audit log</strong> — which actor performed which administrative action, and when.</li>
			<li><strong>Heartbeat pings</strong> — if you enable heartbeat (dead-man's-switch) monitoring, the source IP of each ping is recorded alongside its timestamp so you can tell which job checked in.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			Check history is pruned automatically by a daily scheduled task, and expired sessions are deleted
			on the same run. Deleting a monitor or status page cascades to its related rows.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="card p-6">
		<h2>4. Outbound requests the software makes</h2>
		<p>Your instance reaches the network only to do the job you configured:</p>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>The endpoints you asked it to monitor.</strong> Your monitor's requests carry whatever headers you configured, so the target host sees your Worker's traffic.</li>
			<li><strong>Notification providers you set up</strong> — your own SMTP/email provider, Slack, Discord, or your webhook endpoint. Incident details are sent to them because that is the point of an alert.</li>
			<li><strong>Certificate and DNS lookups</strong> for SSL-expiry and domain checks, where enabled.</li>
			<li><strong>Cloudflare Workers AI</strong>, only if you bind the optional <code>AI</code> binding for incident summaries. The prompt is processed by Cloudflare, not by any other AI vendor, and the feature is inert when the binding is absent.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>Nothing is ever sent to the maintainers of this project.</p>
	</section>

	<section class="card p-6">
		<h2>5. Legal bases and your rights</h2>
		<p>
			For this website, the processing above rests on legitimate interests (operating and securing a
			site) and, for the session cookie, on it being strictly necessary to deliver a service you
			explicitly requested by logging in. Subscriber emails in a self-hosted instance rest on the
			consent that person gave when subscribing.
		</p>
		<p>
			If you're in the UK, EEA, Switzerland, California or a comparable jurisdiction, you have rights
			of access, rectification, erasure, restriction, portability and objection over personal data held
			about you. Because the data lives in the instance you or your provider operates:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>Using someone's status page?</strong> Contact whoever runs it. Unsubscribe links in their emails remove you immediately.</li>
			<li><strong>Running your own instance?</strong> You can service those requests directly against your D1 database.</li>
			<li><strong>Something about this website?</strong> Use the <a href="/contact">contact page</a>.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			We do not sell personal information, and we do not "share" it for cross-context behavioural
			advertising as California defines those terms. There is nothing to opt out of.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="card p-6">
		<h2>6. Children</h2>
		<p>
			UptimeCove is a developer tool and isn't directed at children under 13 (or under 16 where local
			law sets that bar). We don't knowingly collect their data.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="card p-6">
		<h2>7. Security</h2>
		<p>
			Sessions and API keys are stored hashed, monitor URLs and redirect targets are validated to block
			requests to internal addresses, and the dashboard and write APIs sit behind authentication. No
			system is perfectly secure, and this project is young — the
			<a href="https://github.com/iharshalp/UptimeCove/blob/main/SECURITY.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">security policy</a>
			describes the current scope and how to report a vulnerability privately.
		</p>
		<p>
			If you deploy UptimeCove, treat <code>ADMIN_TOKEN</code>, <code>.dev.vars</code> and your remote
			D1/KV state as production secrets.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="card p-6">
		<h2>8. Changes</h2>
		<p>
			Material changes to this page will be reflected in the "last updated" date above. Because the
			site is open source, the full revision history is public in the
			<a href="https://github.com/iharshalp/UptimeCove" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">repository</a> —
			you can diff any change yourself rather than take our word for it.
		</p>
		<p>
			This page describes how the software behaves and is written to be plain and accurate. It is not
			legal advice; if you deploy UptimeCove in a regulated setting, have your own counsel review your
			obligations.
		</p>
	</section>
</PageLayout>
